harness: floor.sh htgen -c400 -d5s h1 unix app=examples/hello.rb, BIN= alternated,
  15 rounds each, interleaved before/after. cflags=-O3 -march=x86-64-v3, NOT native:
  this container migrates between hosts, so a native binary built on one of them is
  an illegal instruction on the next. 6.18.44-fc-v22 x86_64, 4 cpus.
       build     n    median rps       mean        min        max      sd    range
      before    15       526851     491598     340280     590388   18.2%     47%
       after    15       522638     485439     315865     578728   18.1%     50%
  after/before on the medians: 0.992. Welch on the means: t = -0.18, 95% CI on the
  difference -14.9% .. +12.4%. NO DIFFERENCE MEASURED - and at n=15 this harness
  could not have seen one below 13.6% if there had been one.

  The spread is the HARNESS, not the machine. sysbench cpu on the same box, six
  runs under nice -10, ranged 1059.51..1067.05 events/s: sigma 0.24%, range 0.71%,
  which is the stability this tree's notes already claim for it. At -c400 on 4 cpus
  htgen and webmachine are BOTH pegged (server 96-99%, client 97-100% of a core), so
  what the scheduler hands each of them decides the number. floor.sh's own guard
  does not catch this: it refuses when the SERVER has headroom under a pegged
  client, and here neither end has any. A number from this harness on this box is
  worth nothing under ~15%; the load has to come from a second machine.
  before: 340280 341486 356119 397847 428616 509932 516455 526851 531845 532081 557428 576718 581142 586786 590388
  after:  315865 343110 350997 413560 428129 501702 505630 522638 529365 540701 543546 565895 566041 575687 578728
# The first vm_floor run since the scripts were repaired (renamed gem,
# vendored OpenSSL, two flow names). Container numbers: the spread here
# is 13-18% at n=15 and nothing under that is resolvable - these say the
# harness builds and runs, not what the tree costs.
